<statement>
<p>
Consider the following algorithm to convert a list into a two-dimensional array (a matrix).</p>
<p>
def list<underscore/>to<underscore/>matrix(myList, rows):</p>
<p>
  list<underscore/>length = length(myList)</p>
<p>
    if (rows does not divide list<underscore/>length):</p>
<p>
      print("Warning: number of rows must divide the length of the list")</p>
<p>
      return NULL</p>
<p>
  columns = list<underscore/>length / rows </p>
<p>
  myMatrix = a rows x columns array</p>
<p>
  list<underscore/>counter = 0</p>
<p>
  for row in <lbracket/>0, 1, ..., rows - 1<rbracket/>:</p>
<p>
    for column in <lbracket/>0, 1, ..., columns - 1<rbracket/>:</p>
<p>
      myMatrix<lbracket/>row, column<rbracket/> = myList<lbracket/>list<underscore/>counter<rbracket/></p>
<p>
      list<underscore/>counter = list<underscore/>counter + 1</p>
<p>
  return myMatrix
 </p>
<p>
Let <m>\displaystyle{n}</m> denote the length of the input parameter <m>\displaystyle{m}{y}{L}{i}{s}{t}</m>. What is the run-time complexity of the alrorithm, using big-O notation? </p>
<p><ol style="list-style-type:upper-alpha"><li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}\cdot{\log{{\left({n}\right)}}}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({1}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({\log{{\left({n}\right)}}}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}^{{2}}\right)}</m></li> 
</ol></p>
 

</statement>
<solution>
<p><ul><li>D: <m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}\right)}</m></li></ul></p>
</solution>
</myopenmath>

